# Ultrathin Metal–Organic Framework Nanosheets as Nano‐Floating‐Gate for High Performance Transistor Memory Device

## abstract
Metal–organic framework (MOF) is an emerging important class of functional materials in the fields of information storage, wearable electronics and optoelectronic devices. The interaction of electrons or holes with MOFs is important for the systematic exploration of MOFtronics and to investigate the related structure–performance correlation. Herein, MOF flat nanosheets of copper tetrakis(4‐carboxyphenyl)porphyrin with sub‐10 nanometer scale in thickness are employed as the charge‐trapping layer in organic field‐effect floating‐gate transistor memory device fabricated by an air–liquid interfacial assembly and subsequent stamping operation. As charge trapping sites, MOF nanosheets with ultrathin nanoporous arrays significantly improve in comparison with the memory device using its counterpart ligand of tetrakis(4‐carboxyphenyl)porphyrin as the trapping elements. As compared to the reported widely applied nanofloating gate materials of gold nanoparticles, graphene, or macromolecular nanomaterials, a short pulse (≈20 ms) on the device gets a considerable memory window of ≈37.5 V at a programming voltage of ‐80 V, with a retention time longer than 104 s and good ON/OFF ratio of >103. Furthermore, the hybrid structure composed of metal and organic components endows it with electron and hole trapping capability. This work could push forward the fundamental research of organic–inorganic–hybrid electronics in future microelectronic research.

Metal–organic framework (MOF) is an emerging important class of functional materials in the fields of information storage, wearable electronics and optoelectronic devices. The interaction of electrons or holes with MOFs is important for the systematic exploration of MOFtronics and to investigate the related structure–performance correlation. Herein, MOF flat nanosheets of copper tetrakis(4-carboxyphenyl)porphyrin with sub-10 nanometer scale in thickness are employed as the charge-trapping layer in organic field-effect floating-gate transistor memory device fabricated by an air–liquid interfacial assembly and subsequent stamping operation. As charge trapping sites, MOF nanosheets with ultrathin nanoporous arrays significantly improve in comparison with the memory device using its counterpart ligand of tetrakis(4-carboxyphenyl)porphyrin as the trapping elements. As compared to the reported widely applied nanofloating gate materials of gold nanoparticles, graphene, or macromolecular nanomaterials, a short pulse $(\approx20~\mathsf{m s})$ on the device gets a considerable memory window of ${\approx}37.5\:\mathsf{V}$ at a programming voltage of $-80\vee,$ with a retention time longer than $\boldsymbol{\mathsf{10^{4}\mathsf{s}}}$ and good ON/OFF ratio of ${>}70^{3}$ . Furthermore, the hybrid structure composed of metal and organic components endows it with electron and hole trapping capability. This work could push forward the fundamental research of organic– inorganic–hybrid electronics in future microelectronic research.
## 1. Introduction
Metal–organic framework (MOF) is a periodical crystalline hybrid organic–inorganic structure constructed by weak supramolecular interactions and strong coordination bonds with complex microporous hierarchical topology.[1,2] This endows the hybrid materials advantages in systematic structure design, nanoscale synthesis, and the applications in chemical and materials science fields, such as chemical sensors, biomedical, gas storage and catalysis.[3–13] Recently, MOFs are attracting lots of attention in the aspect of physics-related properties, such as porous metal, semiconductors, bistability, and topological insulators.[14–18] For example, Allendorf, Dincă, Nishihara, etc., presented MOFs with good conductivity higher than $0.1\mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}}$ .[17,19–22] And low-k dielectric properties were reported in MOFs of HKUST-1 and ZIF-8 which can serve as efficient electric insulators in modern microelectronic integrated circuits.[18,23,24] Moreover, toward the future industrial applications of flexible organic electronics, including wearable electronic devices, integrated circuits, and implantable chips, it is crucial to develop high-performance low-cost organic memory devices. The basic charge carrier (electrons and holes) trapping, transportation, and releasing process; the controlling of these processes are important fundamental processes in the devices. The typical features of MOF, such as designable hybrid component, ordered micropores, adjustable electronic band structure, tunable charge transport pathways, decent thermal stabilities, and solutionprocessed fabrication adaptability make them promising charge trapping elements in organic memory devices.
NAND flash transistor is a microelectronic device basis element and the operation is based on charge storage on a floating gate or in the gate dielectric. As for organic field-effect floatinggate transistor (OFET) memory, due to the ease of integration, nondestructive reading, and multibit storage possibilities within a single device, it is developed and fabricated by incorporating the flexibility and structure-related properties of organic semiconducting materials, and introducing an additional charge storage layer (floating gate) between the gate dielectric and the organic semiconductor layer.[25–28] The electrons or holes are trapped in the floating gate, and so the device performance are closely related to the reliable trapping in the charge-accepting elements. In previous reports, metallic nanoparticles (Au, Ag, or Al),[29,30] 2D inorganic nanomaterials (graphene, reduced graphene oxide, $\mathbf{M}_{\mathrm{O}}\mathbf{S}_{2}$ nanosheet),[31–33] and organic semiconductors ([6,6]-phenyl-C61-butyric acid methyl ester [PCBM], ferrocene, etc.),[26,34] added into insulating polymers (polystyrene [PS], polymethyl methacrylate, etc.) were employed to incorporate into the charge trapping layer. Furthermore, porous structure in floating gate can benefit the injection of charge carriers and is formed by spin-coating or thermal annealing of polymer insulators. The memory device with porous structured floating-gate may pave a new way to the modulation of the performance or multifunctionalization devices of ion- or gas-stimulus smart memory.[35] It is of great value to investigate solution-processable MOF nanosheets with oriented ordered micropores, proper dielectric properties, and good thermal stability as charge-trapping material for OFET memory.[36,37] There has been no reports on MOF-based OFET memory. And it is of great need to give a thorough insight into the charge-trapping behaviors of MOFs from the aspects of fundamental studies of MOFtronics as well as the technical developments of micro OFET memories.
In this work, we synthesized monodispersed flat ultrathin MOF nanosheets with sub-10 nanometer scale in thickness and ultrathin nanoporous arrays, copper tetrakis(4-carboxyphenyl) porphyrin nanosheets, as the charge-trapping layer. Copper tetrakis(4-carboxyphenyl)porphyrin nanosheets employed as charge-trapping layer is called $\mathrm{{Cu}}$ -CTL thereafter. The 2D MOF material-based OFET memory showed a large memory window on a short writing pulse (less than $20~\mathrm{ms}^{\cdot}$ ). The charge trapping layer can be fabricated on the substrate conveniently on centimeter scale by an air–water interfacial self-assembly and a subsequent stamping solution-processed approach. The trapping ability of $\mathrm{cu}$ -CTL ranks on a highest level in current nanofloating gate materials. The memory characteristics are also significantly improved as compared to the memory device using tetrakis(4-carboxyphenyl)porphyrin (TCPP) film as charge storage layer. The study on the interaction of the charge carriers with MOFs helps for the exploration of solution-processed high-performance OFET electronic devices of information storage and microelectronic circuits beneficial in fundamental research and industrial applications.
## 2. Results and Discussion
The herein employed $\mathrm{\mathsf{Cu}}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{CTL}$ nanosheets were obtained by a sodium dodecyl sulfate (SDS) directed wet-chemical synthetic approach similar to the method we have reported recently.[38] Typically, the mixture of $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}\cdot3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ SDS, and TCPP were dissolved in N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF) and ethanol mixed solvent $\mathrm{'v:v}=3{:}1$ ) in a $20~\mathrm{mL}$ capped vial. After being sonicated for $10\ \mathrm{min}$ , the vial was sealed and heated to $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and then kept for $12\mathrm{~h~}$ . Highly dispersed flat ultrathin nanosheets of Cu-CTL were synthesized as a purple suspended precipitation (see details in Experimental Section). In the fabrication of memory device, the Cu-CTL 2D MOF nanosheets are utilized as the dielectric layer. The memory device is realized by dispersing and assembling the nanosheets on the air–water interface. First, the Cu-CTL nanosheets were well-dispersed in $2~\mathrm{mL}$ of ethanol. Then a small amount of the suspension was spread on the water surface in a $100~\mathrm{mL}$ beaker containing ${\approx}80{\mathrm{-}}90~\mathrm{mL}$ of deionized water. Simultaneously, a large-area continuous uninterrupted film was formed instantly on the water interface. Through a subsequent stamping operation, highly smoothed dielectric layer film can be fabricated by transferring it to the substrate.
As shown in Figure  1, the obtained nanosheets of Cu-CTL are nearly rectangular (Figure  1a). The low contrast of corresponding higher magnification transmission electron microscopy (TEM) image indicates the intrinsic ultrathin thickness of the nanosheets (Figure  1b). Corresponding selected area electron diffraction map confirmed the single crystalline nature of the nanosheets and the diffraction spots in the image correspond to (010) and (100) planes (Figure 1c). High resolution transmission electron microscopy (HRTEM) image of the nanosheets shows that the interplanar distance of the lattice fringe in both directions are 1.68 and $1.69\ \mathrm{nm}$ respectively (Figure 1d). In order to clarify the crystal structure of $\mathrm{{Cu}}$ -CTL, the synchrotron grazing-incidence X-ray diffraction (GIXRD) technique was employed at an incident angle $(\alpha)$ of $0.2^{\circ}$ . By depositing the Cu-CTL nanosheets on $\mathrm{Si}/\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ substrate at a thickness of several hundred nanometers, a high quality X-ray diffraction (XRD) pattern is achieved, and the in-plane and out-of-plane XRD profiles were obtained by Fit2d software (Figure  1e,f). All of the main peaks predicted based on the crystal structure model locate in the in-plane GIXRD profile.[39] In the stacked crystal structure shown in Figure  2a, its unit cell size is $1.6496\ \mathrm{nm}$ , which is consistent with the lattice spacing of $1.68\mathrm{nm}$ in HRTEM image shown in Figure  1d. Moreover, the out-of-plane diffraction showed one peak at ${\approx}19.5^{\circ}$ , which is a typical characteristic XRD pattern of the 2D MOF and is consistent with its interlayer spacing of $0.902\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Figure S1, Supporting Information, structure facing $a$ or $b$ axis).[40] UV–vis absorption spectra of the Cu-CTL nanosheets and TCPP are performed to get an insight to the coordination interactions between the metal ions and the porphyrin ligands. As seen in Figure S2, Supporting Information, the porphyrin ligand shows its characteristic absorption feature with a strong absorption Soret band at $419\ \mathrm{nm}$ and four weak absorption $\mathsf{Q}$ bands in the range of $500{-}670~\mathrm{nm}$ As for the Cu-CTL nanosheets, the Soret band red-shifted to around $433~\mathrm{nm}$ and the obvious two peaks for Q band proved that Cu ions coordinate into the center of the porphyrin ring. Then, the nanosheets were spread into a macroscale continuous smooth thin film on an air–water interface, which were transferred to the $\mathrm{Si}/\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ wafer substrate by stamping for the fabrication of the OFET memory device shown in Figure  2b (See  Experimental Section). The flatness characteristics for the MOF nanosheets synthesized ensured a homogeneous high-quality film on the centimeter scale, which benefit a qualified transistor. It is noted that the dielectric layer can also be prepared by dip-coating of nanoparticle dispersion onto the $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}/\mathrm{Si}$ substrate, but the obtained film is not smooth enough for a qualified device. Figure 2c shows the large macroscopic dense thin film formed on the water surface. The scanning electron microscope (SEM) image of the nanosheet film after $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ annealing in Figure  2d confirmed the film is composed of a large number of nanosheets. From the AFM images, it can be seen that the thickness of the nanosheet is about $8\pm1~\mathrm{{nm}}$ ,  which corresponds to a layer number of about $9\pm1$ (Figure $^{2\mathrm{g},\mathrm{h}}$ , one interlayer space is $0.9\ \mathrm{nm}$ ). The cross-sectional image of the membrane in Figure  2e shows the thickness of the fabricated film is about $40\ \mathrm{nm}$ . The contact angle of the film is $114^{\circ}$ with respect to water, indicates a hydrophobic nature of Cu-CTL nanosheets film on the surface of the silicon wafer (Figure 2f ). Finally, the thermogravimetric analysis (Figure S3, Supporting Information) shows that the ultrathin nanosheets exhibit good thermal stability and will not degrade before $300~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , ensuring that the nanosheets will not be damaged by subsequent thermal annealing of the nanosheets at about $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Based on the above, the prepared Cu-CTL thin film can be well adapted and applied in OFET memory.
By depositing a semiconductor layer of pentacene with a thickness of about $50\ \mathrm{nm}$ on the film of Cu-CTL dielectric membrane and deposition of patterned Au electrodes, nanofloating-gate transistor memory based on Cu-CTL nanosheet was fabricated. As shown in Figure 2b, the bottom-gate top-contact OFET memory device possessed $60~\mathrm{nm}$ thick Au thin film as the source and drain electrodes, and heavily doped p-type Si as the gate electrode. A blocking layer of PS can also be added between Cu-CTL and pentacene to prohibit the trapped charged from escaping into pentacene. To investigate its charge trapping abilities of holes and electrons, Cu-CTL and Cu-CTL with PS layer-based devices as well as the TCPP-based device are fabricated and studied. All scans of source–drain current $\left(I_{\mathrm{DS}}\right)$ versus gate-source voltage $(V_{\mathrm{GS}})$ are at a fixed source-drain voltage $(V_{\mathrm{DS}})$ of $-30{\mathrm{~V}}.$ When a negative programming $V_{\mathrm{GS}}$ of $-80\mathrm{V}$ was applied under dark condition for $0.02\mathrm{~s~}$ the transfer curves shifted toward the negative bias direction, which served as the writing process. Then after applying a positive erasing $V_{\mathrm{GS}}$ of 50 or $80\mathrm{~V~}$ (or under illumination), the transfer curves shifted to the positive bias direction and finally returned to the initial state, corresponding to the erasing process. As for Cu-CTL-based OFET device with PS, the representative output and transfer characteristics showed a p-type field-effect transistor behavior with good saturation property (Figure  3a and Figure S4, Supporting Information). The measured field-effect hole mobility $(\mu)$ and $I_{\mathrm{on}}/I_{\mathrm{off}}$ current ratio were $0.37\mathrm{cm}^{2}\mathrm{V}^{-1}\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ and $10^{5}$ , respectively. The memory behavior of the device was measured by applying an appropriate gate bias of $-80\mathrm{~V~}$ for $0.02\mathrm{~s~}$ to generate $V_{\mathrm{TH}}$ shifts. The programming curve shows a threshold voltage of $-44.36~\mathrm{V},$ and the erasing curve shows threshold voltage of $-6.88\mathrm{~V},$ and thus the memory window $(\varDelta V_{\mathrm{TH}})$ of the OFET memory is 37.48 V (Figure 3b). An endurance test of the OFET memory was performed, and it maintained a well-defined programming/erasing switching process for at least 100 write/read/erase/read (WRER) cycles, showing a stable OFET memory device (Figure 3c), and the retention characteristic was kept for at least $10^{4}$ (Figure 3d). Generally, organic semiconducting OFET memory generally needs at least 1  s to stimulate a good voltage shift.[30,32,41–46] Whereas in comparison with the typical employed 2D nanomaterials of reduced graphene oxide and $\mathsf{M o S}_{2}$ , and macromolecules and gold nanoparticles (Table S1, Supporting Information), during no more than 0.02 s pulse time, Cu-CTL herein ranks the highest charge trapping performance level, MP value (the percentage of Memory window/Programming voltage, e.g., 37.48/80 for $\mathsf{C u-C T L})$ of which gets 0.47 (on time scale of $0.1\mathrm{~s~}$ , ferrotin nanoparticles gets 0.14;[45] CuPc-PS4 gets 0.36 [CuPc-PS4: star-shaped polystyrene with a copper phthalocyanine core];[42] 6FDA-DBA-SP gets 0.37 [6FDA-DBA-SP: poly(3,5-benzoic acid hexafluoroisopropylidene diphthalimide][43]). Besides, Zn-TCPP/PVPy (PVPy: polyvinyl pyrrolidone) gets 0.29 and 0.27 at a programming/ erasing voltage of $\pm5$ and $\pm8\mathrm{~V~}$ in a three-terminal synaptic device for simultaneous learning and signal transmission.[47] Based on the above, it can be demonstrated that a high charge trapping ability exhibited in the nanosheets of Cu-CTL.
Next, the characteristic curves of memory device based on pristine Cu-CTL are tested. 2D and 3D AFM topographies of the pentacene surface of the typical device are shown in Figure S5, Supporting Information. The pentacene surface of Cu-CTL with PS layer exhibits a smaller roughness value of $\mathrm{Ra}=2.1\ \mathrm{nm}$ , whereas roughness of the pentacene surface with Cu-CTL without PS exhibits a roughness of $8.0\ \mathrm{nm}$ . PS layer can fill in spatial nonuniformities of the surface and the roughness of the $\mathtt{C u}$ -CTL film improved with PS layer eliminated. For pristine Cu-CTL-based device, applying a negative programming $V_{\mathrm{GS}}$ of $-80\mathrm{~V~}$ for 0.02  s (Figure  4a), the initial curve switched to negative direction from the threshold voltage of $-8.65$ to $-22.34\mathrm{~V}.$ It can be successfully erased back with $4.74\mathrm{~V~}$ by applying an inverse positive $V_{\mathrm{GS}}$ . The $\Delta V_{\mathrm{TH}}$ of the OFET memory is $27.08{\mathrm{~V}}.$ When applying a positive programming $V_{\mathrm{GS}}$ of $+50{\mathrm{~V~}}$ for 0.02  s (Figure S6a, Supporting Information), the initial curve switched to positive direction from the threshold voltage $(V_{\mathrm{TH}})$ of $-1.95$ to $10.50~\mathrm{V}.$ By applying an inverse negative $V_{\mathrm{GS}}$ , it can be successfully erased backwards with $V_{\mathrm{TH}}$ of $-13.30\mathrm{~V},$ leading to a $\Delta V_{\mathrm{TH}}$ of $23.80\mathrm{~V}.$ Contrarily, the Cu-CTL-based device can also be written at a negative programming $V_{\mathrm{GS}}$ of $-50\mathrm{~V~}$ for $0.02\mathrm{~s~}$ , and the transfer curves easily goes back toward the initial at an inverse erasing $V_{\mathrm{GS}}$ of $+50$ V (Figure S6b, Supporting Information). Next, the endurance test for WRER cycles maintains a well-defined programming/erasing switching process, which indicates a reliable memory for the pristine Cu-CTL based device without PS layer (Figure  4b). The test curve of $\Delta V_{\mathrm{TH}}$ versus the programming time is shown in Figure  4d. In the transfer curves at the programming time of 0.02, 0.04, 0.06, 0.08, 0.1, and $\mathrm{~1~s~}$ , the $V_{\mathrm{TH}}$ transfer values doesn't get an obvious change. This suggests that the memory can be written fast at a short time of 0.02 s for Cu-CTL, and the unchanged memory window suggests that an even faster programming speed may be predicted. When the charges are trapped by the floating gate layer, saturation can be reached very quickly. Besides, the retention characteristic shows that the difference between $I_{\mathrm{on}}$ and $I_{\mathrm{off}}$ lasts greater than ten times after $10^{4}$ (Figure 4c). Although the retention is not as good as the device with PS layer on it, the WRER cycles still suggests a reliable writing/erasing process. As for the TCPP/PS deposited film, the memory window after applying of a negative programming $V_{\mathrm{GS}}$ of $-80\mathrm{V}$ for 0.02 s is $\approx7.19{\mathrm{~V~}}$ , which is lower than that of Cu-CTL/PS film $(\approx37.48{\mathrm{~V}})$ . Besides, it cannot be erased back to the initial curves on an inverse positive erasing $V_{\mathrm{GS}}$ voltage, even under light illumination to stimulate exciton injection (Figure  4e). Moreover, the devices based on TCPP deposited film with/without PS layer cannot be written on positive voltage (Figures 4f and 5a). Therefore, different from pristine Cu-CTL, which can be conveniently electronically written on a positive voltage (under darkness, Figure S6a, Supporting Information), pristine TCPP film-based memory cannot be electronically written on a positive voltage (Figure 5a). And after a negative written process, it also cannot be electronically erased back, except under auxiliary light illumination (Figure 5b,c).
The possible charge trapping mechanism was proposed. First, according to the electrochemical cyclic voltammetry $I{-}V$ curves, the band levels of Cu-CTL and TCPP are measured and calculated (Figure  5d, see Experimental Section). As seen in Figure 5e, the lowest unoccupied molecular orbital (LUMO) levels of pentacene, Cu-CTL, and TCPP are $-3.0$ , $-3.36$ , and $-2.72\ \mathrm{eV},$ respectively; while their highest occupied molecular orbital (HOMO) levels are $-5.0$ , $-5.15$ , and $-5.62\ \mathrm{eV}.$ As for electrons, Cu-CTL has a similar slightly lower LUMO level $(-3.36~\mathrm{eV})$ than pentacene $(-3.0\ \mathrm{eV})$ , more suitable for the electron injection as compared to TCPP; while the HOMO energy levels of Cu-CTL and TCPP are at the same direction for the injection of holes. According to Fowler–Nordheim tunneling process in OFET device, when applying a negative gate voltage, during the programming process accompanied by hole-injection, as seen in Figures 3b and 4a (or Figure S6b, Supporting Information), the hole carriers produced in pentacene molecules can transfer into Cu-CTL and be trapped there. The trapped hole charges form an internal electric field partially offsetting the external applied electric field. This led to low-conductivity state in channel, resulting in the negative shift of the transfer curve. During the electronically erasing operation, with a positive bias applied, the holes can be detrapped from Cu-CTL to surrounding pentacene. This results in highconductivity state and the transfer curve shifts back (Figure 5g). In contrast, as applying a positive programming gate pulse on the Cu-CTL memory (Figure S6a, Supporting Information), electrons are induced from the electrode and trapped by Cu-CTL, which form an internal electric field, leading to lowconductivity state in channel and the transfer curve shifting toward the positive direction. Whereas with an erasing negative gate pulse applied, the electrons can be detrapped from Cu-CTL. This results in high-conductivity state and the transfer curve shifts back. As for electrons, they can easily enter Cu-CTL nanosheets from pentacene, so as to realize electrical writeread-erase cycles (Figure 5g, Cu-CTL). With PS layer on it, the positive erasing process can be realized with light illumination (Figure  3b and Figure S8, Supporting Information). But since TCPP has a higher LUMO level (Figure 5g, TCPP), which prohibits the injection of electron carriers, it cannot be written on positive gate pulse (Figure  5a). Furthermore, during the positive erasing operation, extra light illumination is needed (Figure  5b,c), which generates photo-generated excitons and provide electrons recombining with the trapped holes. After adding PS layer on it, even a failure occurred in light illumination plus positive electrical erasing operation (Figure 4e).
As mentioned in the above context, there is a considerable difference between the memory window for the Cu-CTL/PS and TCPP/PS memory device, which is 37.48 and 7.19 V, respectively, both at the same thickness of the charge trapping layer (Figures 3b and 4e). Porphyrin is a class of $\pi$ conjugated macrocycle compound which tend to trap holes in OFET devices. The charge-storage density $(\Delta n)$ can be estimated by the following equation[27]
$$
\Delta n=\frac{\Delta V_{\mathrm{TH}}\ C i}{e}
$$
where ${\mathrm{C}}i$ is the whole capacitance per unit area of the gate dielectric layer and $e$ is the elementary charge (for details, see figure caption in Figures S10 and S11, Supporting Information). The charge density of the device based on Cu-CTL/PS film is $2.21\times10^{\bar{12}}$ , obviously higher than that of TCPP/PS film, $3.79\times10^{11}$ . The enhancement can be possibly attributed to two reasons. One is related to the different morphology for these films (Figure  5f). As in memory device, the charge carriers may prefer to be trapped in the curved or defected sites.[25] For Cu-CTL nanosheets film, it has higher contacting surface of the interface with pentacene than the deposited TCPP film. As shown in the chart of Figure  5f, more charge carriers can be trapped in the edge sites of the stacked nanosheets on the interface. But it should not be the main reason for the contribution of the enhancement, since many 2D nanosheets materials, such as r-GO and $\mathsf{M o S}_{2}$ flakes, although their films are also of stacked nanosheets, do not show a superior charge ability as compared to other materials of gold nanoparticles and macromolecules in OFET memory (Table S1, Supporting Information).[32,33] Moreover, the memories with pristine TCPP and Cu-CTL represent similar memory window, 13.56 and $12.90\mathrm{~V~}$ for holes (Figures  5c and  4a), respectively, although there is a higher roughness for Cu-CTL than TCPP (Figures S5 and S7, Supporting Information, and notes therein). Except the morphology, the enhancement possibly also relates to the different molecular spatial distribution structure of Cu-CTL and TCPP. As seen in Figure S9, Supporting Information, Soret band of TCPP in the UV–vis of the TCPP film locates at $429{\mathrm{nm}}$ , which is red-shifted as compared to the TCPP diluted solution. This indicates that the TCPP molecules are in a J-aggregate mode in the film.[48] The aggregation makes porphyrin molecules orderly stacking, which leads to larger Coulombic interaction energies and smaller band gaps and induces the red-shift in the absorption band. In the porphyrin J-aggregates, the molecules stacked side-by-side by a perpendicular interspace of $3\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ via $\pi-\pi$ stacking interactions. In contrast, in the microporous Cu-CTL, TCPP molecules arranged over against each other periodically with an interspace of $4.05\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ , which odiously overpassed the effective distance of $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking interactions of TCPP, which may be beneficial for hole tunneling and trapping in the inside of Cu-CTL (Figure  5f), and leading to a higher charge trapping ability. Nano pores were reported to be able to improve the storage performance.[49] The exploration of ordered porous arrays with sizes below $10\mathrm{nm}$ is promising, but it is prohibited in currently applied nano floating-gate materials of GO, $\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ , and metallic nanostructures because of their space structure limitation.[49] Herein, Cu-CTL proved promising charge trapping abilities for MOFs. The structure of Cu-CTL MOF nanosheets endows it good abilities in holes and electrons fast trapping with stable WRER cycles and long retention time in the OFET memory, and the proposed nano-floating-gate of the herein flat 2D MOF further provides a promising low-cost, solution-processed fabrication of high-quality multilayered OFET memory device.
## 3. Conclusion
Herein, ultrathin porous flat 2D MOFs of copper tetrakis(4-carboxyphenyl)porphyrin were synthesized and fabricated as nanofloating gate of the OFET memory device. Studies showed that it is a promising charge trapping material of holes and electrons. It is demonstrated that a short pulse $(\approx20~\mathrm{ms})$ ) gets a considerable memory window $(\approx37.5~\mathrm{V})$ at a programming voltage of $-80\mathrm{~V},$ with stable WRER cycles, good ON/OFF ratio, and long retention time. It can trap electrons efficiently and reliably without the auxiliary stimulus of light illumination. During a relative short gate pulse of no more than $0.02\mathrm{~s~}$ , it is superior to the reported widely applied nanofloating gate materials of gold, graphene, or macromolecular nanomaterials. The 2D MOF nanosheets were synthesized by a SDS-directed solution synthetic method, providing a flat ultrathin structure and beneficial for the successful solution-processed air–liquid interfacial self-assembly and stamping process, thus ensuring high-quality film fabrication for the memory device. The good charge trapping abilities could be attributed to the defects on the nanosheet film, and moreover, the micropores promoting hole tunneling and charge trapping ability which improves the charge density and leads to a large memory window. The investigation on the interaction of electrons and holes with MOF would be sure to put forward the fundamental research of organic–inorganic–hybrid electronics in future microelectronic development. Further to take account of the chemi–physical properties and intrinsic flexibility of MOF nanosheets, the proposed nano-floating-gate of flat 2D MOF should make a hopeful contribution to the emerging newconcept flexible electronic devices of information storage, logic circuits, wearable electronic devices, and even smart machines.
## 4. Experimental Section
Materials: Pentacene $(99\%)$ and TCPP $(97\%)$ were purchased from Tokyo Chemical Industry Co., Ltd. Copper nitrate hydrate and SDS $(99\%)$ was bought from Alfa Aesar. Ethanol, PS $(\mathsf{M w}=250\ 000\ \mathsf{g\ m o l^{-1}})$ was purchased from Sigma Aldrich Chemical Reagent. DMF, toluene, and ethanol were from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd. All of the materials were used without further purification.
Synthesis of $C u\mathrm{-}C T L$ Nanosheets: The ultrathin flat nanosheets were first synthesized successfully by the SDS-directed solution synthetic method the authors' recently reported.[38] Typically, copper nitrate hydrate $(\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}.3\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}$ , $3.8~\mathsf{m g},$ ) and SDS $(70.0\ m g)$ were dissolved in $72~\mathsf{m L}$ of DMF and ethanol mixture $(\mathsf{v};\mathsf{v}=3;\mathsf{l}$ ) in a $20~\mathsf{m L}$ capped vial. Then, TCPP $(4.0\ m g,\ 0.005\ m m o l)$ in $4m L$ of DMF and ethanol mixed solvent $(\mathsf{v};\mathsf{v}=3;\mathsf{l}$ ) were added dropwise under vigorous stirring. After that, the solution was sonicated for $70~\mathrm{min}$ . The vial was sealed and heated to $80~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ and then kept for $12\mathrm{~h~}$ . Then it was allowed to cool to room temperature. The precipitation was centrifuged at $10\ 000\ \mathrm{~r~}\mathsf{m i n}^{-1}$ and washed for three times by DMF and ethanol to remove residual SDS and metal ions. Finally, the obtained product was dispersed in $2m L$ of ethanol for film fabrication.
Fabrication of $C u$ -CTL-Based OFET Memory: The wafer of a $300-\mathsf{n m}$ -thick thermally grown silicon dioxide covered highly doped n-type Si was used as bottom gate substrate. Then the wafer was cut into $7.5~{\mathsf{c m}}\times1.5~{\mathsf{c m}}$ in size. After that, the wafer was cleaned through sequentially rinsing with acetone, ethanol, and ultrapure water, respectively, in an ultrasonic bath for 10 min. After blowing with a steam of nitrogen, it was dried in the oven at ${\mathsf{120}}\ {\mathsf{^{o}C}}$ for $30~\mathrm{min}$ .
Film Fabrication of Cu-CTL: The thin film was fabricated by an air– liquid interfacial self-assembly and stamping process.[40,50] First, the previously prepared nanosheets were well-dispersed in $2m L$ of ethanol sufficiently. A small amount of the suspension was aspirated with a $3m L$ disposable pipette, and the resulting pellet was spread in a $700~\mathsf{m L}$ beaker containing $80-90~\mathrm{mL}$ of deionized water. Then a largearea continuous film was formed instantly on the air–water interface. The thin film was easily transferred to a $\mathsf{S i}/\mathsf{S i O}_{2}$ wafer substrate $(\mathsf{S i O}_{2}$ thickness: $300\ n m\Biggr,$ ) by a simple stamping operation. The transferred Cu-CTL film was rinsed gently with deionized water, left to dry, and further blown with nitrogen, and then dried in a vacuum oven at $120^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $30~\mathsf{m i n}$ .
Fabrication of OFET Memory Device: The $\mathsf{S i}/\mathsf{S i O}_{2}$ substrate with $C u-C T L$ was transferred to a spin coater and $2~\mathsf{m g}\mathsf{m}\mathsf{L}^{-1}$ of PS dissolved in toluene was spin-coated at a speed of $2000\ r\mathsf{m i n}^{-1}$ for $30~\mathsf{s}$ . Next, the wafer was annealed on a hotplate in the glove box at $80~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $30\mathrm{\min}$ A PS layer with the thickness of about $76\ n m$ was obtained. Then the pentacene was thermally deposited at a rate of $0.2\mathring{\mathsf{A}}\mathsf{s}^{-1}$ under a base pressure of $5\times10^{-4}$ Torr layer to form a $50~\mathsf{n m}$ -in-thickness film. Finally, source and drain Au electrodes $(60\ n m)$ were thermally deposited through a shadow mask with the channel length $(L=700~\upmu\mathrm{m})$ and width $(\mathsf{W}=2000~\upmu\mathrm{m})$ , respectively. As for the TCPP-based device, the compound of TCPP was thermally deposited on $\mathsf{S i}/\mathsf{S i O}_{2}$ at a rate of $0.2\mathring{\mathsf{A}}\mathsf{s}^{-1}$ under a base pressure of $5\times10^{-4}$ Torr layer to form a $40\ n m$ -in-thickness film. The following deposition of PS, pentacene, and electrodes were as above procedures. All electrical measurements were carried out under ambient conditions at room temperature. The electrical characteristics of the fabricated transistor memory devices were measured using a Keithley 4200 semiconductor parametric analyzer under ambient conditions in dark or under illumination by a white light-emitting diode with the wavelength of $400{\-}700\ \mathsf{n m}$ and light intensity of $188~\mathsf{m w}\mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ from the top. Note that all the device electrical characteristics curves were collected in dark if without introduction of illumination.
Instruments and Characterization: FESEM images were captured on a Hitachi S-4800 field emission SEM. TEM experiments were performed using a JEM-2100 electron microscope (JEOL, Japan). AFM image was recorded by a Dimension Icon atomic force micrographs (Bruker, Germany). Thermal gravimetric analysis measurements were collected on a Pyris 1 TGA under argon atmosphere (PerkinElmer, American). The X-ray diffraction pattern was obtained at beamline BL14B1 of the Shanghai Synchrotron Radiation Facility using X-ray with a wavelength of 0.6887  Å. The detailed information about beamline BL14B1 could be found in the reported literature.[51] The thickness of spin-coated or vapor deposited films were measured by a Bruker Dektak XT stylus profiler. Cyclic voltammetry curves were conducted on the CHI660E system with a typical three-electrode system of platinum sheet working electrode, platinum wire counter electrode, and silver/ silver nitrate $({\sf A g/A g}+)$ reference electrode at room temperature. All electrochemical experiments were carried out under a nitrogen atmosphere at room temperature and performed on the solution of the sample on a glassy carbon electrode measured in $0.1\ m o l\ L^{-1}$ acetonitrile solution of tetrabutylammonium hexafluorophosphate at a sweeping rate of $0.1\ \vee\ \mathsf{s}^{-1}$ .  The  freshly  prepared Cu-CTL nanosheets were dispersed in $2m L$ of ethanol, and a proper amount of dispersion liquid was dropped onto a platinum carbon electrode and dried under nitrogen to form a thin film. According to the redox onset potentials of the cyclic voltammetry measurements, the HOMO/LUMO energy levels of the materials were estimated based on the reference energy level of ferrocene (4.8  eV below the vacuum), $E_{\mathsf{H O M O}}\ =$ $-(E_{\mathrm{onset}}^{\mathrm{Ox}}\mathrm{~-~}0.08~\mathsf{V})~-~4.8~\mathsf{e V}_{\mathrm{{\mathrm{{\iota}}}}}$ $E_{\mathsf{L U M O}}=-(E_{\mathsf{o n s e t}}^{\mathsf{R e d}}-0.08~\mathsf{V})-4.8~\mathsf{e V}$ . The dielectric properties were evaluated using HP4194 impedance analyzer. Corresponding curves of dielectric constant versus frequency were recorded from $70~\mathsf{k H z}$ .
